     1	"""Stage one: everything the CPU can do, and nothing that costs money.
     2	
     3	The pipeline splits at the only expensive step. This stage renders each part, finds the boxes
     4	geometrically from the ruled lines, finds the text lines inside them, reads the three fields
     5	tesseract is good enough for -- the EPIC, the section header and the closing totals -- and packs
     6	the four labelled lines into composites for Cloud Vision.
     7	
     8	Everything it produces is what stage two needs and nothing more:
     9	
    10	- **composites**, the images Vision is billed for. Repacking cuts a rendered page from 15.5 MP
    11	  to 30% of that, which is 3.3x fewer images: $345 for the state against about $105.
    12	- **placements**, every tile's rectangle inside its composite. Vision answers in the composite's
    13	  coordinates and a word has to come back to the box it was cut from; without this, stage two
    14	  would have to re-derive the geometry and could silently disagree with the geometry that
    15	  produced the image.
    16	- **a manifest**, one row per tile carrying ac, part, page, box, band, rectangle, section and
    17	  the source sha, so a reading can be put in the right row and traced to exact source bytes.
    18	- **side reads**, the EPIC and serial per box and the part's printed closing total, which is the
    19	  only number in the source that the extracted rows can be measured against.
    20	
    21	It costs about 142 ms of CPU per box -- 985 core-hours for all 126 constituencies, roughly $8 of
    22	spot CPU or five days on a laptop -- and nothing at all in API charges.
    23	"""
    24	
    25	from __future__ import annotations
    26	
    27	import json
    28	import time
    29	from dataclasses import dataclass, field
    30	from pathlib import Path
    31	from typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional, Sequence
