11. The reconciliation checks use incompatible cardinalities instead of the same part-key set.

   [electors/reconcile.py:74](/Users/soodoku/Documents/GitHub/assam_elex_rolls_2026/electors/reconcile.py:74), [electors/reconcile.py:90](/Users/soodoku/Documents/GitHub/assam_elex_rolls_2026/electors/reconcile.py:90), [electors/reconcile.py:110](/Users/soodoku/Documents/GitHub/assam_elex_rolls_2026/electors/reconcile.py:110)

   `parts_complete()` compares only the number of distinct row parts; `measured_coverage()` compares only two collection lengths; `rows_against_printed()` iterates only total-map keys.

   Reproduced wrongly passing input: row parts `{1, 2}`, `published_parts=2`, and totals `{1: 1, 3: 0}`. Part 2 is never measured, phantom part 3 matches zero rows, yet all three external checks pass and `judge().ok` is true.

12. Box-to-row verification can be absent or partial without failing.

   [electors/reconcile.py:146](/Users/soodoku/Documents/GitHub/assam_elex_rolls_2026/electors/reconcile.py:146), [electors/reconcile.py:213](/Users/soodoku/Documents/GitHub/assam_elex_rolls_2026/electors/reconcile.py:213)

   An empty map skips the check. A nonempty map checks only its own keys and never requires coverage of every row or published part.

   Wrongly passing input: rows contain parts 1 and 2, but `boxes_per_part={1: 1}`. The check reports “all kept”; part 2 has no box evidence at all. This can hide lost supplement rows because closing totals cover only the main list.

13. `done()` and `ready()` still disagree about stage version.

   [electors/stage1.py:278](/Users/soodoku/Documents/GitHub/assam_elex_rolls_2026/electors/stage1.py:278), [electors/stage2.py:237](/Users/soodoku/Documents/GitHub/assam_elex_rolls_2026/electors/stage2.py:237)

   `done()` requires `stage1_version == STAGE1_VERSION`; `ready()` does not inspect it.

   Wrongly passing input: a structurally present part with an old `stage1_version`. `done()` returns false while `ready()` returns true. If re-preparation fails or a row cache exists, `run_ac()` can consume the stale part that `done()` explicitly rejected.

